      "description": "Explore regional Iranian sweets through Isfahan’s gaz, Qom’s sohan and rice-flour cookies, with context on ingredients, preparation and local variation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-regional-identity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional specialty is not an exclusive recipe",
        "content": "Iran’s sweets are best approached as related traditions rather than a catalogue in which every recipe belongs exclusively to one city. Encyclopaedia Iranica documents cookies associated with particular regions that also appear elsewhere under different names or with modified recipes. A regional reputation therefore identifies an important connection, rather than proving a single birthplace or an unchanged formula. [s1]\n\nA useful comparison begins with texture and technique: a confection built around beaten egg whites differs substantially from a cooked brittle or a flour-based cookie. These distinctions make the subject more intelligible than translating every sweet simply as a pastry. Gaz, sohan and nan-e berenji illustrate these contrasting approaches. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "isfahan-gaz",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Isfahan: gaz",
        "content": "Gaz is a confection particularly associated with Isfahan. Iranica’s account describes a preparation using gaz-angobin, a sweet substance obtained from the Khansar area, together with egg whites, sugar and pistachios or almonds. The mixture is heated and worked into a paste before being shaped and cooled. [s2]\n\nPacking pieces in loose flour helps keep them from sticking and protects them against moisture. That flour is part of the handling method, rather than evidence that gaz is a baked biscuit. Iranica also emphasizes that confectioners guard their own procedures and proportions. Its description should consequently be read as a documented traditional preparation, not an ingredient guarantee for every contemporary package. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qom-sohan",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Qom: sohan",
        "content": "Sohan provides a different point of comparison. TasteIran’s account associates it with Qom and describes a cooked confection beginning with wheat-sprout powder and water. Its preparation then incorporates ghee, sugar and egg yolks, followed by rosewater, saffron and cardamom; flattened portions receive pistachios and almonds. [s3]\n\nThis account makes the importance of process clear: sustained heating, stirring and the development of the mixture’s consistency precede shaping. It is a useful description of a reported preparation, although an individual confectioner may use a different formulation. Claims about royal encounters or the precise invention of sohan require separate historical evidence and are not necessary to understand the sweet itself. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cookies-and-gifts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cookies, variation and travel gifts",
        "content": "Nan-e berenji means rice-flour cookies. Iranica identifies Kermanshah, Qazvin and Shiraz as places renowned for them and describes a dough containing rice flour, powdered sugar, rosewater, eggs and oil. It also notes their brittleness and popularity as travel gifts. The same entry describes northern kolucheh with a walnut or coconut filling and distinguishes household preparation from confectionery-shop production. [s1]\n\nTaken together, these examples suggest questions worth asking about any regional sweet: what gives it structure, which ingredients provide its aroma, and how is it packed? For a purchase, the actual ingredient declaration matters more than a generalized recipe. A city name offers cultural context; it does not establish the contents of a particular box. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
