    "seo": {
      "title": "Travel Infrastructure in Iran: Accommodation, Transport and Booking | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iranian accommodation and travel services, heritage reuse, payment constraints and gaps in current operating information."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "services-as-a-connected-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What makes a destination usable",
        "content": "Travel infrastructure is the system that connects an attraction with a place to stay, a means of arrival and a workable transaction. This section separates hotels, ecolodges, travel agencies, tour guides, online booking systems and tourist transport because each answers a different question. A room description does not establish transport availability; a listing does not establish that an international customer can complete payment.\n\nIran’s heritage record offers a long perspective on these connections. UNESCO describes caravanserais as roadside inns providing shelter, food and water. Their locations responded to water availability, geography and security. The historical lesson is practical: hospitality has always depended on supporting resources and routes, rather than buildings alone. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accommodation-and-heritage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hotels, ecolodges and the reuse of older buildings",
        "content": "Historic architecture is part of the accommodation story, but heritage status and present-day hospitality are separate matters. UNESCO’s Persian Caravanserai documentation distinguishes archaeological remains from properties retaining a resting or accommodation function. It also identifies maintenance and management needs. Inclusion in a heritage property therefore does not establish that a particular caravanserai accepts overnight guests. [s1]\n\nYazd provides a more specific example of adaptation. UNESCO documents hotels and restaurants operating in rehabilitated existing structures, with principal physical elements retained and interventions minimized. This places accommodation within the continuing life of a historic city rather than outside it. [s2]\n\nFor this directory, hotels and ecolodges warrant separate treatment, but neither label should be treated as evidence of a particular conservation or environmental outcome. An accommodation profile needs to establish what the individual property actually provides. The inspected sources do not support nationwide claims about ecolodge certification, energy performance or consistent facilities.\n\nThe wider context in Yazd is an urban fabric shaped by limited water and climatic adaptation. UNESCO describes qanats, underground channels bringing water to settlements, alongside thick earthen walls, courtyards and wind-catchers. These features explain the buildings’ significance; they do not by themselves establish the comfort, accessibility or current condition of a guest room. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "agencies-guides-and-booking",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Organization, interpretation and booking",
        "content": "The agency, guide and booking-system branches should make responsibilities explicit. An agency profile needs to identify who contracts for the service and handles changes. A guide profile needs evidence of the person’s or organization’s actual offer. A booking-system profile needs to distinguish an enquiry, a reservation request and a confirmed purchase. These are editorial criteria for evaluating services, not findings that all providers follow the same process.\n\nPayment access is a material constraint. Canada’s Iran advisory, updated before this page’s cutoff, reports that foreign credit cards are not accepted and that ATMs serve local banking. It also identifies restrictions on international transfers. This supports a narrow conclusion: an international visitor should not infer payment compatibility from the existence of an online listing. It does not mean Iran lacks domestic electronic banking or card payments. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transport-and-operating-conditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Transport capacity is different from an operating service",
        "content": "Canada’s advisory describes a relatively developed highway system while also identifying variable road conditions and safety concerns. Physical connectivity and journey quality are different measures. An infrastructure overview should therefore distinguish the existence of a road or terminal from a verified service, its timetable and the conditions under which it operates. [s3]\n\nThe same advisory, last updated on October 13, 2026, advised Canadians to avoid all travel to Iran and reported airline suspensions, while noting that some commercial flights and overland crossing options remained available. These were dated, nationality-specific official statements, not a verified transport schedule for every reader. No route, frequency or border opening is guaranteed here. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-the-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains to be measured",
        "content": "A defensible national assessment would distinguish accommodation establishments, rooms, beds and occupied nights, then examine transport and service availability separately. Growth in one measure cannot establish improvement in the others. Comparable annual figures and their original reporting periods were not verified during this research, so this page includes no capacity chart.\n\nThe supported picture is consequently strongest on heritage accommodation and transaction constraints. Completing it requires primary evidence on service classifications, geographic coverage and operating networks. Until that evidence is available, the branches below provide a structure for further research rather than a national inventory of verified providers.",
